2. Being aggrieved of and dissatisfied with the impugned award/judgment, appellant has assailed its legality, propriety and correctness on the following grounds:-
(i) that respondent No. 1 (petitioner before MACT Jammu) age more than 65 years,
retired police personnel filed a petition before MACT Jammu in regard to injury suffered by him on account of an accident met to him on 19.03.2003 at Model Town Jammu after being hit by Maruti Car bearing No. PB-02AC/4844 due to rash and negligent driving of its driver (respondent No. 3) whereby respondent No. 1 suffered fracture of his left leg and remained admitted in GMC Jammu up to 04.04.2003, it was claimed in the claim petition that after retirement petitioner was doing the business but without disclosing the nature, place of business or other details of business or any proof or evidence;
(ii) that the respondent Nos. 2 & 3 failed to appear before the Tribunal who were proceeded ex-parte, appellant before the tribunal filed detailed objections pleading various defences for dismissal of the petition viz; that the car was being plied in breach of policy conditions as the driver of the car was not holding a valid and effective driving license and was not authorized to drive light motor vehicle;
(iii) that respondent No. 1 examined himself alongwith his 4 witnesses PWs Harbans Kour, Narinder Kour (wife), Rajinder Singh (son) and Dr. Dara Singh, and the tribunal accepted the petition of respondent No. 1 and passed an Award dated 26.02.2010 whereby directing the appellant to pay the respondent No. 1 Rs.
7,17,200/- with interest at the rate of Rs. 7.5 per annum from the date of filing of the petition till the payment is made;
(iv) that the Tribunal below committed illegality in assuming the income of respondent No. 1 at Rs. 4,000 per month though there was no evidence at all regarding the profession and income of respondent No. 1, moreso, respondent No 1 was a retired government employee and was drawing pension and therefore it could not be said that there was loss of income to respondent No. 1, the finding recorded by the tribunal to the said extent requires to be set aside ;
(v) that an award of Rs. 1,50,000/- each under the heads (i) Pain and Sufferings & (ii)
loss of amenities of life to respondent no. 1, is unjust and unreasonable, as respondent 1 had suffered only fracture in left leg, but the tribunal has awarded such huge amount contrary to the law;
